---
/**
 * The privacy page (#422): what Google Analytics 4 collects on genome-os.org, which cookies it
 * sets and when (opt-in in the EEA, the UK, Switzerland, Quebec and Turkey; opt-out elsewhere,
 * with Global Privacy Control honoured), how long they and the data last, and how to change the
 * choice. Every build publishes it; a build without analytics (the /genomeOS/ fallback, local dev)
 * says so first.
 */
import BrandName from '../components/BrandName.astro';
import CookieSettings from '../components/CookieSettings.astro';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { gaMeasurementId } from '../lib/ga.mjs';

const analytics = gaMeasurementId() !== null;
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="How genome-os.org uses Google Analytics, which cookies it sets and when, and how to change your choice."
>
  <PageIntro
    note="Privacy"
    title="What we measure, and what we don't."
    lede="genomeOS uses Google Analytics to see which pages and Atlas datasets people use, so we can improve them. There are no ads, and we never send what you look at on the map."
  />

  <section class="contribution-detail" aria-label="How analytics works">
    <div class="section-shell contribution-detail__grid">
      {
        !analytics && (
          <article data-no-analytics>
            <h2>This copy of the site</h2>
            <p>
              This copy of <BrandName /> loads no analytics and sets no cookies.
              The rest of this page describes genome-os.org, which does.
            </p>
          </article>
        )
      }
      <article>
        <h2>Cookies and your choice</h2>
        <p>
          Google Analytics 4 sets two first-party cookies, <code>_ga</code> and <code
            >_ga_&lt;container&gt;</code
          >, which tell one visit from the next. Each lasts up to two years
          unless you turn analytics off. Whether they are set depends on where
          you are:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong
              >European Economic Area, UK, Switzerland, Quebec and Turkey:
              opt-in.</strong
            >
            This includes the EU’s overseas regions, Åland, the Channel Islands, the
            Isle of Man and Gibraltar. Devices set to Eastern Time in Canada, which
            covers Ontario as well as most of Quebec, are asked too. Until you choose
            <strong>Accept</strong>, the site doesn’t load Google Analytics, so
            nothing is sent to Google. Visitors in these time zones see a short
            Accept or Decline prompt on their first visit. If Google places you
            in these regions but your device is set to a time zone elsewhere,
            Google Analytics loads without setting cookies and sends cookieless
            pings, which Google uses only to estimate totals, until you choose.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Everywhere else: opt-out.</strong> The cookies are set by default
            unless you turn them off, or your browser sends a <a
              href="https://globalprivacycontrol.org/"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer">Global Privacy Control</a
            > signal, which we honour as an opt-out.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Your choice is kept in your browser’s local storage, not in a cookie,
          and applies on every page until you change it. While analytics is off,
          the pages you open don’t load Google Analytics at all. Turning it off
          also removes the analytics cookies.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h2>What is collected</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>the pages you visit, by path only, never the query after it;</li>
          <li>
            the page or site that linked you here, also without its query;
          </li>
          <li>
            your approximate location (city, region and country), worked out
            from your IP address; Google Analytics 4 does not log or store IP
            addresses;
          </li>
          <li>
            your device, browser, operating system, language and screen size.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>The Atlas also counts three actions, by public name only:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            which dataset is opened, only once you have chosen <strong
              >Accept</strong
            > or turned analytics on yourself, wherever you are, because the datasets
            a person opens can hint at a health interest;
          </li>
          <li>switches between the globe, map and perspective views;</li>
          <li>which inspector is opened, for a map cell or an observation.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          They never include the places you look at or click, or anything else
          about you.
        </p>
        <p>
          Google Analytics keeps this visit-level data for two months, then
          deletes it. Only the aggregate reports, such as page counts, are kept
          after that.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h2>What is not</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>No advertising, and no ad personalisation.</li>
          <li>
            Google signals are off, so visits are not linked to Google accounts
            or across devices.
          </li>
          <li>No names, accounts or other personal details.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Map imagery and the Atlas’s optional data lookups load straight from
          their providers, such as OpenStreetMap and Cesium ion, under their own
          policies.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article id="your-choice">
        <h2>Changing your choice</h2>
        <p>
          Every page has a small cookie icon in a bottom corner. In the Atlas it
          sits under the map controls on a wide screen, and at the end of the
          data credits on a phone. It opens your cookie settings, with a switch
          for analytics cookies that you can change at any time. The <strong
            >Cookie settings</strong
          > link at the foot of the site and documentation pages opens the same panel.
          Blocking Google Analytics in your browser or content blocker also stops
          it; the site works the same without it.
        </p>
        {
          analytics && (
            <p>
              <CookieSettings class="privacy-cookie-settings" />
            </p>
          )
        }
        <p>
          Google runs the service, and the <BrandName /> maintainers see only its
          aggregate reports. Google’s <a
            href="https://policies.google.com/privacy"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">privacy policy</a
          > and its page on <a
            href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >how Google uses information from sites that use its services</a
          > apply to it.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>

<style>
  :global(.privacy-cookie-settings) {
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: 0.45rem 1rem;
    border: 1px solid rgb(112 230 255 / 55%);
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
    background: rgb(112 230 255 / 10%);
    color: var(--color-starlight);
    font: inherit;
    font-weight: 650;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  :global(.privacy-cookie-settings:hover) {
    background: rgb(112 230 255 / 20%);
  }
</style>
